#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180636} :activity/id #uuid "d591686e-75aa-506f-aade-856d6dea793a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049495 :topic/id #uuid "f9324acf-7bff-528d-bb24-b67ba475ce6a" :topic/math-academy-id 3746} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180660 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca3cabae-8ebe-5879-b608-9d0ebdb233fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4a7f682-c223-50f1-a3eb-6b1f79aad9b0" :step/math-academy-id 637169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :question/id #uuid "02ae40ba-a02c-5025-9597-1968322f2421" :question/m